# Caddy reverse proxy. Chosen over Nginx for one reason: zero-config Let's
# Encrypt with automatic renewal (PLAN.md §15.1).
#
# `{$DOMAIN}` is substituted from the environment — compose passes it from the
# env file. Set it to the public hostname; Caddy provisions a certificate for
# exactly that name on first request. For a local test with no public DNS, set
# DOMAIN to `:80` and Caddy serves plain HTTP without attempting ACME.
{$DOMAIN} {
	# The app listens on the port baked into the image as PORT (3000). This
	# is NOT the port `npm run start` uses locally — that is 80, which only
	# governs a local `next start`. The container never runs `next start`; it
	# runs `.next/standalone/server.js`, which reads PORT/HOSTNAME from the
	# environment. Changing it means changing all three of: the Dockerfile
	# `ENV PORT`, the compose healthcheck, and this line.
	reverse_proxy app:3000

	# ── Deliberately NO security headers here ────────────────────────────
	# It is tempting to add Caddy's usual `header` block with CSP, HSTS,
	# X-Frame-Options and friends. Do not.
	#
	# The app emits the whole set itself: the static headers come from
	# `next.config.ts` and the Content-Security-Policy is minted per request
	# in `proxy.ts`, carrying a fresh nonce that Next stamps onto the script
	# tags it renders.
	#
	# A second CSP header does not replace the app's. Per the CSP spec a
	# browser enforces *every* policy it receives and a resource must pass
	# all of them, so adding one here yields the intersection. The proxy
	# cannot know the request's nonce — it is generated inside the app after
	# Caddy has already forwarded the request — so any policy written here
	# necessarily blocks the nonced *inline* scripts Next uses to ship the
	# RSC payload and boot the client. Hydration dies, and the failure looks
	# like "the app renders but nothing is interactive".
	#
	# The rest of the set has the same problem in milder form: duplicated
	# headers produce whichever value the browser picks first, so the app's
	# real policy silently stops being the one in effect. Configure headers
	# in one place — the app. See docs/reference/security.md for the
	# inventory of what it already sends.

	# Compression is a proxy concern, not an app concern — safe to add here.
	encode zstd gzip

	# Structured access logs to stdout, so `docker logs proxy` matches the
	# JSON the app and worker already emit via pino.
	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}
}
